# ── Chapter 13 fidelity pins: "The One Thing Taken" ──────────────────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/13-the-one-thing-taken.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # the real check — the line above cannot see
#                        # the cross-file reconciliation or the prose guards
#
# TRIGGER FIRED. This file used to record "there is no release" as an absence and
# said: "If a release rule is ever added, the closing section must be rewritten."
# It was, and it has been. Release is now Article 6's `~free` guard plus Article
# 1's `free -> person`, and the closing two sections are pinned below.
#
# One claim here is still an ABSENCE and cannot be queried: "there is no duration
# in it anywhere, no term, no length, nothing that counts". `verify.sh` holds
# that one — a digit in an enacted line fails the build.
#
# The other: "Nothing depends on it" — travel appears in the constitution only as
# a rule head. Test the rule BODIES, since a bare grep matches the head too:
#   awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /travel/' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli
# must stay EMPTY. `verify.sh` runs it with a positive control.

:expect-pins 26

# "Hano was convicted ... and cannot move freely."
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? travel(Hano).
# => FALSE

# The audit, item by item — everything else Hano keeps
? person(Hano).
# => TRUE

? expresses(Hano).
# => TRUE

? decide(Hano, Ballot).
# => TRUE

? false(Hano).
# => FALSE

? lose(Points, Hano).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Hano).
# => TRUE

# "the difference between Hano and Jala, who did the same thing to the same
#  person and was never convicted"
? injure(Jala, Ivo).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Jala).
# => FALSE

? travel(Jala).
# => TRUE

# Voiding is not confinement: Bela lost credibility and still moves
? false(Bela).
# => TRUE

? travel(Bela).
# => TRUE

# ── "And it may end" / "What leaving costs" ──────────────────────────────────
# LAST in the file: this block asserts a release and changes Hano for everything
# after it. Nothing follows.

# "Somebody records that it is over — that is one of the things the world may say"
free(Hano).

# "the machinery that was holding a person stops holding them. Movement returns.
#  The placement stops. The marker that was watching for isolation goes quiet."
? prisoner(Hano).
# => FALSE

? travel(Hano).
# => TRUE

? err(Hano, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# "The person keeps every entitlement they had ... and the body that owes it
#  still owes it"
? person(Hano).
# => TRUE

? entitled(Hano, event { dwell() }).
# => TRUE

? owe(State, Provision, Hano).
# => TRUE

# "Both of them arrive only for prisoners. So when a sentence is finished, both
#  stop." — the chapter's sharpest claim, and the two pins that carry it.
? expresses(Hano).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Hano).
# => FALSE

# ── The Hano/Jala lists, pinned item by item ─────────────────────────────────
# "Four differences, and three of them run the wrong way." An earlier draft said
# "Everything else on both lists is identical", which was false in four places.
# Pinned as pairs so the claim cannot drift back.
? expresses(Jala).
# => FALSE

? dwell(Jala).
# => FALSE

? fit(Jala, Homestay).
# => FALSE

? err(Jala, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# and Jala is genuinely unconvicted — the control for all four
? prisoner(Jala).
# => FALSE
